---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const moneyCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'gap', label: 'output gap (recession − / boom +)', min: -5, max: 5, step: 0.5, value: -2, unit: '%' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'Taylor-rule rate = 2 + inflation + 0.5·gap − 1', expr: '2 + 2 + 0.5*gap - 1', unit: '%' },
    { label: 'rate move suggested per meeting', expr: 'min(0.5, max(-0.5, 0.25*gap))', unit: 'pp' },
  ],
};

const moneyQuizCfg = {
  questions: [
    {
      q: 'The central bank raises its policy rate today. When will the effect on inflation mostly arrive?',
      choices: [
        'Within days — rates are direct price control',
        'Over 12–18 months: the rate cascades through bank funding, mortgages and business loans into spending, hiring and price-setting — Friedman\'s "long and variable lags", which is why central banks steer by forecast, not by today\'s reading',
        'Never — rates do not affect inflation',
        'Only after legislation',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'Transmission is a chain: policy rate → interbank and deposit rates → borrowing costs → investment, housing and consumption → output gap → wage and price setting. Each link takes months, and the long lag is why policy must be forward-looking — and why critics can always accuse banks of fighting the last war.',
    },
    {
      q: 'When quantitative easing buys government bonds, what actually happens?',
      choices: [
        'The government destroys the bonds',
        'The central bank creates bank reserves (its own IOUs) and swaps them for bonds held by financial institutions — pushing down long-term yields and easing financial conditions; the money stays in the banking system as reserves, not in people\'s pockets',
        'Cash is posted to every household',
        'Taxes are abolished',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'QE is an asset swap, not a helicopter: reserves are held by banks at the central bank and pay interest. It works through portfolios (sellers rebalance into other assets, lowering their yields) and signalling — powerful when rates are pinned at zero, less clearly powerful otherwise, and its unwinding (quantitative tightening) is the 2020s experiment.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Why is central-bank credibility said to make disinflation cheaper?',
      choices: [
        'Because credibility lowers interest rates only',
        'Because wage- and price-setters act on expected inflation: if workers and firms believe the bank will hit 2%, they build 2% into their plans and inflation falls without a deep recession; lost credibility (the 1970s) forces inflation out through unemployment instead',
        'Because journalists write nicer stories',
        'Because credible banks can print more money',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'Expectations are the battleground: every contract embeds a guess about future prices. The Fed anchored expectations near 2% through 2021–2024 — which is why, despite inflation reaching 9%, wage-price spirals stayed contained and the eventual disinflation did not need a Volcker-scale recession. Credibility is capital, built over decades and spent in crises.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Volcker raised US rates to nearly 20% in 1980–81, causing a deep recession, and inflation fell from 14% to under 4%. The lesson economists draw is:',
      choices: [
        'High rates are always the answer',
        'The cost of restoring credibility after it is lost: once expectations had un-anchored, only a demonstrated willingness to inflict pain would reset them — a reference case for prevention being cheaper than cure',
        'Recessions are good',
        'Inflation cannot be fought',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'The Volcker disinflation is the field\'s canonical costly lesson: killing entrenched inflation took two recessions and unemployment near 11%. The institutional design of the modern era — independent banks, explicit targets, transparent communication — is essentially an attempt to never need another Volcker.',
    },
  ],
};

const mpMandateCloze = {
  "text": "Most rich-country central banks target inflation at about %%0%%, alongside %%1%% employment. Their main tools are the policy %%2%%, balance-sheet operations, and the credibility of their word.",
  "answers": [
    [
      "2%",
      "two per cent"
    ],
    [
      "maximum"
    ],
    [
      "rate"
    ]
  ],
  "hint": "The mandate is price stability plus maximum employment; the rate is the main dial."
};

const mpNeutralPlace = {
  "prompt": "With inflation at 2%, a 2% target and a zero output gap, what neutral nominal policy rate does the Taylor rule give? Place it.",
  "labels": [
    "0%",
    "2%",
    "4%",
    "6%",
    "8%"
  ],
  "scale": {
    "min": 0,
    "max": 8
  },
  "answer": 4,
  "tolerance": 0.2,
  "why": "Neutral real rate (2%) + inflation (2%) + 0.5×(0) + 0.5×(0) = 4%. That is the setting when the economy is at target and at capacity."
};

const mpIndependenceSort = {
  "prompt": "Who does what? File each responsibility.",
  "buckets": [
    "central bank",
    "government"
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "label": "sets the inflation target",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "chooses the policy-rate path",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "issues the currency",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "runs fiscal policy",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "lends freely in a panic",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "appoints the central bank's leadership",
      "bucket": 1
    }
  ],
  "why": "Independence separates the target (a political choice) from the path to it (a technocratic one) — the design that insulates rate-setting from the election cycle."
};

const mpChannelsMatch = {
  "prompt": "Pair each transmission channel with its example:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "borrowing costs",
      "right": "mortgages and investment plans are postponed"
    },
    {
      "left": "wealth effects",
      "right": "higher discount rates lower house and equity prices, so holders feel poorer"
    },
    {
      "left": "exchange rate",
      "right": "higher rates strengthen the currency, cheapening imports and squeezing exporters"
    },
    {
      "left": "credit supply",
      "right": "banks tighten lending standards"
    }
  ],
  "why": "All four channels fire at once; the chain takes roughly 12–18 months to reach inflation."
};

const mpTransmissionOrder = {
  "prompt": "Put the transmission chain in order, from decision to inflation:",
  "items": [
    "The central bank changes the overnight policy rate",
    "Interbank rates and banks' funding costs adjust",
    "Mortgage, business-loan and savings rates follow",
    "Households and firms postpone or reshape spending",
    "Demand, hiring and price-setting respond — roughly 12–18 months later"
  ],
  "why": "Friedman's long and variable lags: the bank must act on forecasts because the evidence arrives long after the decision."
};

const mpTaylorEst = {
  "prompt": "Using the Taylor rule with a 2% neutral real rate, 9% inflation, a 2% target and a +2% output gap, what policy rate does the recipe prescribe?",
  "unit": "%",
  "answer": 15.5,
  "factor": 1.1,
  "why": "2 + 9 + 0.5×(9−2) + 0.5×2 = 2 + 9 + 3.5 + 1 = 15.5%. Actual peaks were lower, but the direction matched.",
  "context": "the rule is a benchmark, not a robot"
};

const mpQeBranch = {
  "root": "The policy rate is at zero, inflation is below target and the economy is in recession. Trace the central bank's options.",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "text": "Conventional cuts are exhausted. What does the bank reach for?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Buy long-term bonds with newly created reserves (QE)",
          "next": "qe",
          "feedback": "Yes — QE pushes down long-term yields and signals low rates for long."
        },
        {
          "label": "Raise the policy rate to defend credibility",
          "next": "raise",
          "feedback": "That tightens an already weak economy."
        },
        {
          "label": "Do nothing and wait for the recovery",
          "next": "wait",
          "feedback": "With the zero bound binding, waiting risks a deeper slump."
        }
      ]
    },
    "qe": {
      "text": "QE is an asset swap: the bank creates reserves and buys bonds. Sellers rebalance into other assets, long yields fall, and the commitment signals low rates. The money stays inside the banking system."
    },
    "raise": {
      "text": "Raising rates at the zero bound would deepen the recession; credibility is defended by hitting the target, not by tightening into weakness.",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "So what is the tool?",
          "next": "qe",
          "feedback": "The balance sheet."
        }
      ]
    },
    "wait": {
      "text": "At the zero bound, inaction can let expectations drift below target and make the slump self-reinforcing.",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "What else can it do?",
          "next": "qe",
          "feedback": "Use the balance sheet."
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  "why": "When the policy rate hits zero, QE becomes rate policy by other means — an asset swap that lowers long yields and signals future accommodation."
};

const mpCredibilityJudge = {
  "statements": [
    {
      "s": "If the public believes the 2% target, wage- and price-setters build it into contracts, so disinflation costs less unemployment.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "Anchored expectations do some of the tightening for the central bank."
    },
    {
      "s": "The Volcker disinflation of 1980–82 was cheap in jobs because the Fed's credibility was intact.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "It cost two recessions and unemployment near 11% — credibility had to be rebuilt the hard way."
    },
    {
      "s": "Forward guidance manages expectations without moving the policy rate today.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "'Talk is policy': the cheapest rate decision is the one the market makes on the bank's behalf."
    },
    {
      "s": "Anchored expectations can do some of the work of an actual rate rise.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "The 2021–24 episode suggested expectations did more work than joblessness in bringing inflation down."
    }
  ]
};

const mpRatesSpot = {
  "prompt": "Find the line where the interest-rate reasoning goes wrong:",
  "lines": [
    "The central bank sets its overnight policy rate.",
    "It also sets mortgage, corporate-bond and credit-card rates directly.",
    "Those rates are market prices built from the expected path of the policy rate plus risk premia.",
    "That is why a rate announcement can move long-term yields without any action.",
    "If investors doubt the bank's credibility, long yields can rise even as the policy rate falls."
  ],
  "errorLine": 1,
  "correction": "It sets one overnight rate; every other rate is a market price built from expectations and risk premia.",
  "why": "The bond market's daily audit of credibility is why announcements move long-term rates."
};

const mpTradeoffCompare = {
  "prompt": "Two directions, two costs — drag the divider:",
  "before": {
    "label": "fighting inflation",
    "text": "raise rates; demand cools and unemployment rises — the Volcker arithmetic"
  },
  "after": {
    "label": "fighting recession",
    "text": "cut rates; demand recovers, but cheap money inflates asset prices and rewards leverage"
  },
  "why": "No setting escapes the trade-off: price stability costs jobs, and stimulus risks finance. The distribution of both is uneven."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/economics/intermediate/monetary-policy-central-banks">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate Economics lesson, and it assumes the two foundational lessons of
    this subject: money and inflation (what money is, who creates it, and what a price level
    is) and supply and demand (how markets clear, and what shifts them). This lesson adds the
    steering wheel: how one overnight interest rate, moved by an independent institution,
    travels through every market you met there.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain what a central bank is, and why its independence is an anti-inflation device;</li>
    <li>trace the transmission chain from a policy-rate decision to prices, and say how long it takes;</li>
    <li>read the Taylor rule both as a recipe and as a benchmark markets can be surprised by;</li>
    <li>describe what quantitative easing actually swaps, and what unwinding it means;</li>
    <li>explain why expectations are half the fight, and name the trade-offs no rate setting escapes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Central banks steer the economy with <strong>interest rates</strong>. Raise rates: borrowing
    costs climb, spending cools, inflation eases. Cut rates: credit flows, spending rises,
    employment recovers. Their mandate in most rich countries: <strong>price stability (~2%
    inflation) plus maximum employment</strong> — and their main tools are the policy rate,
    balance-sheet operations (quantitative easing), and the credibility of their word.
    The steering wheel is not directly connected to the wheels: rates move an economy
    through a chain of financial decisions spread over a year or more, which is why
    central banking is equal parts mechanics and theatre — the mechanics of the
    transmission chain, and the management of what 330 million people <em>expect</em> to
    happen to prices. This page covers what central banks are and why they are
    independent, how the policy rate travels through the economy, the inflation-targeting
    framework and the Taylor rule, quantitative easing and its unwinding, expectations
    as half the fight, and the trade-offs that no setting escapes.
  </p>
  <Widget no="1" caption="fill the blanks — the mandate and the tools" widget="cloze" config={mpMandateCloze} />

  <Widget no="2" caption="the Taylor rule — the famous recipe mapping the economy's temperature into an interest rate" widget="sliderLab" config={moneyCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> slide the output gap from recession to boom and watch both
    readouts move together — the deeper the recession, the harder the recipe leans toward cuts,
    and the suggested per-meeting move is capped, because central banks step in quarter-point
    increments rather than leaps. Notice also what the rule is not: a robot. Real committees
    deviate, and the deviations are where the judgement — and the explanation to markets —
    lives.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="mark the neutral setting" widget="place" config={mpNeutralPlace} />

  <p>
    The Taylor rule (1993): set the rate near 2% (neutral) plus inflation, tightened by 0.5
    points for each point of overheating. It describes what central banks roughly do — and gives
    markets a benchmark to be surprised by. When inflation ran to 9% in 2022, the rule said
    raise fast; the Fed did.
  </p>

  <h2>What a central bank is — and why it is independent</h2>
  <p>
    A central bank is the economy's bank: it issues the currency, holds banks'
    reserves, lends to banks in emergencies (<strong>lender of last resort</strong> —
    the 19th-century Bagehot doctrine: lend freely, at a penalty rate, against
    good collateral, in a panic), and sets the short-term interest rate at which
    the banking system borrows from it. Its political design is as important
    as its mechanics: <strong>independence</strong> — governments set the target;
    unelected technocrats choose the path to it — exists because the temptation
    to boost the economy before elections produces inflation (the 1970s
    provided the natural experiment: politically dominated banks everywhere,
    inflation everywhere). Time and again, countries that granted independence
    (New Zealand pioneered inflation targeting in 1989–90, the Bank of England
    gained operational independence in 1997) saw inflation expectations fall
    and stay lower. The mandate split matters: the Fed has a <strong>dual
    mandate</strong> (price stability and maximum employment), the ECB a
    hierarchical one (price stability first), the Bank of England a 2% CPI
    target with a letter-writing duty when inflation strays more than a
    point — institutional details that shape every rate decision.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="who does what?" widget="sort-buckets" config={mpIndependenceSort} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the central bank creates all the money in the economy —
  that the notes it issues are the money supply. In modern economies the bulk of money is
  commercial-bank deposits, created when banks make loans (the Bank of England has stated
  this plainly in its own publications): the central bank supplies the reserves and the
  currency that the system settles in, while everyday money is mostly private bank IOUs.
  This is why the phrase "the central bank prints money" is a metaphor that misleads more
  than it explains.</p>

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> a central bank banks the banks, lends freely in panics at a penalty, and sets one short-term rate — with independence designed to keep pre-election booms from turning into inflation. Next: how that single overnight rate moves an entire continent of borrowing decisions.</p>

  <h2>The transmission chain: how a rate decision moves a continent</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>policy rate</strong> (Fed funds rate, Bank Rate, the deposit rate) is
    overnight money between banks — and everything else keys off it. The
    cascade: the policy rate sets interbank lending rates; those set banks'
    funding costs; funding costs set mortgage rates, business loan rates,
    credit card rates, and the return on savings. Higher rates then work
    through several channels at once: <strong>borrowing costs</strong> (mortgages
    and investment plans are postponed), <strong>wealth effects</strong> (higher
    discount rates lower asset prices — equities, houses — making holders
    feel poorer and spend less), <strong>exchange rates</strong> (higher rates
    attract capital, strengthening the currency, cheapening imports and
    squeezing exporters), and <strong>credit supply</strong> (banks tighten
    lending standards). The whole chain takes <strong>roughly 12–18 months</strong>
    to reach inflation — Friedman's "long and variable lags" — which
    creates the central banker's permanent dilemma: they must act on
    forecasts, defend actions whose effects are not visible for years,
    and accept that by the time the evidence arrives, conditions may
    have changed. The 2022–2023 cycle sharpened the point: rates went
    from near zero to over 5% in about 18 months — the fastest
    tightening since the 1980s — with the full effects still landing
    years later.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="pair each channel with its example" widget="match" config={mpChannelsMatch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a rate rise fights today's inflation, so its effect
  should show up within weeks and the bank should "wait and see" before acting. The chain —
  policy rate to funding costs to borrowing decisions to spending to price-setting — takes
  roughly 12-18 months, which is why central banks must act on forecasts and why they
  invariably look, to critics, like they are fighting the last war. Waiting for inflation
  to be visible in the data means the medicine arrives after the disease has spread into
  wage- and price-setting.</p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="from rate decision to inflation" widget="order" config={mpTransmissionOrder} />

  <h2>Inflation targeting and the Taylor rule</h2>
  <p>
    The modern framework is <strong>inflation targeting</strong>: announce a target
    (2% nearly everywhere — a number with folkloric origins in New Zealand's
    1989 design, now entrenched by inertia), explain deviations publicly,
    and move rates according to a reaction function. The <strong>Taylor rule</strong>
    (John Taylor, 1993) is the famous recipe: policy rate ≈ neutral real rate
    (about 2%) + current inflation + 0.5 × (inflation − target) + 0.5 ×
    (output gap) — tighten when inflation runs hot <em>and</em> when the economy
    runs above capacity. <strong>A worked reading:</strong> with inflation at 2%,
    target 2%, and a zero output gap, the rule says about 4% nominal — the
    "neutral" setting. With 9% inflation and a +2% gap (2022), it says
    2 + 9 + 0.5×7 + 1 = over 15% — describing the aggressive tightening
    that followed (actual rates peaked lower, but the direction matched).
    No bank follows the rule mechanically — judgement, financial stability
    and the zero floor intervene — but it disciplines debate: it gives
    markets a benchmark to be surprised by, and makes deviations
    explainable ("we see a larger output gap than the rule assumes").
    The deeper economics underneath is the <strong>Phillips curve</strong>: inflation
    responds to the output gap (the unemployment lesson's trade-off), so
    a central bank buying lower inflation is buying it with slower growth
    — the exchange rate of the trade is the steepest political question
    in macroeconomics.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="call the Taylor-rule rate" widget="estimate" config={mpTaylorEst} />

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> the target is announced, the reaction function is public, and the Taylor rule disciplines the debate — but all of it presumes the policy rate still has room to move. What happens when the rate reaches the floor?</p>

  <h2>Quantitative easing: the balance sheet as a tool</h2>
  <p>
    When the policy rate hit the <strong>zero lower bound</strong> (2008, 2020),
    conventional policy was exhausted — so central banks reached for the
    balance sheet. <strong>Quantitative easing (QE)</strong>: the central bank
    creates bank reserves (its own liabilities) and uses them to buy
    long-term government bonds (and, at the Fed, mortgage-backed
    securities) from financial institutions. The Fed's balance sheet
    grew from about <strong>$900 billion before 2008 to roughly $9 trillion
    by 2022</strong>. The mechanism is a portfolio swap: sellers rebalance
    into other assets, pushing down long-term yields across the economy,
    while the sheer commitment signals that rates stay low for long —
    QE as rate policy by other means. The common misreading — "they
    printed money and gave it away" — misses that the money is bank
    reserves, held inside the banking system and paying interest, not
    household cash. The 2020s added the reverse gear,
    <strong>quantitative tightening (QT)</strong>: letting bonds mature (and,
    at some banks, actively selling), shrinking balance sheets —
    monetary policy in reverse, an experiment whose full effects are
    still being measured. Negative policy rates (ECB, Japan, mid-2010s)
    pushed the same frontier further: charging banks to hold reserves,
    with murky evidence and clear limits (cash in vaults is the floor).
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="the zero lower bound — trace the options" widget="branch" config={mpQeBranch} />

  <h2>Expectations: half the fight happens in heads</h2>
  <p>
    Wages are set on what workers expect prices to do; prices are set on
    what firms expect wages to do; and the loop closes on itself — which
    is why <strong>expectations</strong> determine how costly disinflation is. If
    the public believes the bank will hit 2%, contracts embed 2%, and
    inflation drifts there without mass unemployment. If credibility
    breaks — as in the 1970s, when two oil shocks and political
    pressure broke the Fed's word — expectations un-anchor, and
    inflation can only be forced out through recession: Arthur
    Burns's accommodative 1970s Fed versus <strong>Paul Volcker's</strong>
    1980–82 squeeze (rates near <strong>20%</strong>, two recessions,
    unemployment near 11%, inflation down from 14% to under 4%) is
    the reference case of credibility's price. The modern toolkit
    manages expectations as deliberately as it manages rates:
    <strong>forward guidance</strong> ("rates will stay low until unemployment
    falls below 6%"), inflation reports, dot plots, press conferences —
    "talk is policy", because the cheapest rate decision is the one
    the market makes on your behalf.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="true or false — credibility and expectations" widget="judge" config={mpCredibilityJudge} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the central bank sets all interest rates."
  It sets one overnight rate; every other rate — mortgages, corporate
  bonds, credit cards — is a market price built from expectations of the
  future path of that rate plus risk premia. This is why rate
  <em>announcements</em> can move long-term rates without any action, and why
  markets can fight the bank: if investors expect more inflation, they
  demand higher long-term yields whatever the overnight rate is —
  the bond market's daily audit of the central bank's credibility.</p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="find the interest-rate slip" widget="spot-error" config={mpRatesSpot} />

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> expectations decide whether disinflation is cheap or catastrophic, and the modern toolkit manages them as deliberately as it manages rates. Even with expectations anchored, no setting escapes the trade-offs — there are no free moves.</p>

  <h2>The trade-offs that no setting escapes</h2>
  <p>
    Monetary policy has no free moves. <strong>Fighting inflation costs
    jobs:</strong> the Phillips curve converts tighter policy into slower growth
    and higher unemployment — the Volcker arithmetic. <strong>Fighting
    recessions risks finance:</strong> a decade of near-zero rates inflated
    asset prices (housing, equities), rewarded leverage, and — the
    "zombification" critique — kept unproductive firms alive. <strong>The
    distribution is uneven:</strong> easing raises asset prices (owners gain)
    while tightening raises unemployment (workers pay) — so monetary
    policy redistributes by class, whatever its neutral intent.
    <strong>Exchange rates bind smaller economies:</strong> a small open
    economy's rates move its currency, importing or exporting other
    countries' shocks. The institutional answer to all of these is not a
    better dial but transparency about the dial's cost — which is why
    the inflation-targeting era made its trade-offs explicit in every
    forecast round.
  </p>
  <Widget no="11" caption="two directions, two costs — drag the divider" widget="compare" config={mpTradeoffCompare} />

  <h2>Lender of last resort: Bagehot's three rules</h2>
  <p>
    The central bank's oldest tool is not a dial but a promise, and Walter
    Bagehot wrote its manual during the 1866 Overend Gurney panic:
    in a crisis, the central bank should <strong>lend freely</strong> (stop the
    contagion), <strong>at a penalty rate</strong> (so banks use it only when
    genuinely illiquid, not for cheap funding), <strong>against good
    collateral</strong> (so the lender of last resort funds solvent-but-
    illiquid banks, not insolvent ones). The three clauses are the
    whole dilemma: lend without the penalty and you subsidise risk;
    lend without collateral and you rescue the insolvent; refuse to
    lend at all and a solvent system dies of a queue. Every financial
    rescue since is a variation: 2008's facilities were Bagehot with
    wider collateral lists; 2023's bank failures tested the same
    clauses with faster depositors (mobile banking moving billions
    in hours). The tool sits oddly beside inflation targeting —
    crisis lending expands the balance sheet exactly when the
    dial says stand down — which is why the two functions are
    run as separate rooms of the same institution, and why
    the collateral rules, not the headline amounts, are
    where the accountability lives.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>The central bank raises its policy rate today. When does the effect on inflation mostly arrive, and why?</li>
    <li>When quantitative easing buys government bonds, what actually changes hands, and who ends up holding what?</li>
    <li>Why is credibility said to make disinflation cheaper?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Over roughly 12-18 months: the rate cascades through bank funding, mortgages and business loans into spending, hiring and price-setting — Friedman's long and variable lags, which is why banks steer by forecast and always look, to critics, like they are fighting the last war. (2) The central bank creates bank reserves — its own IOUs — and swaps them for bonds held by financial institutions; the money stays inside the banking system as reserves, not in people's pockets, working through portfolio rebalancing and signalling. (3) Because wage- and price-setters act on expected inflation: if they believe the 2% target, they build it into contracts and inflation falls without a deep recession — lost credibility, as in the 1970s, forces inflation out through unemployment instead.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A central bank issues the currency, banks the banks, lends in panics (freely, at a penalty, against collateral) and sets one overnight rate.</li>
    <li>Independence is an anti-inflation device: governments set the target, unelected technocrats choose the path.</li>
    <li>Transmission runs rate, to funding costs, to borrowing and spending, to prices — over roughly 12-18 months.</li>
    <li>The Taylor rule benchmarks the rate against inflation and the output gap; judgement deviates, and the deviations are explainable.</li>
    <li>QE is an asset swap that creates bank reserves, not helicopter cash; QT is the reverse gear.</li>
    <li>Expectations are half the fight: anchored, disinflation is cheap; un-anchored, it costs a Volcker.</li>
    <li>No setting escapes trade-offs — jobs for price stability, asset booms for growth, and an uneven distribution either way.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    fiscal-policy-public-debt is the sibling lever: the interest rate this lesson moves is the
    same one in the debt-dynamics arithmetic, and the two policies act on the same economy at
    the same time. unemployment-labour-markets takes up the Phillips-curve trade-off behind
    every rate decision, and the foundational money-and-inflation lesson is worth rereading
    once you know who actually creates the everyday money.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The 2021–2024 stress test.</strong> "Transitory" was the mistake heard
    round the world: supply shocks, stimulus and shifting demand met a
    framework built for demand shortfalls. The inflation peaked at 9% in
    the UK, 9.1% in the US (June 2022), and the subsequent tightening
    brought prices down without (through 2024) the deep recession the
    Phillips curve predicted — reopening the curve-steepness debate
    (anchored expectations did more work than joblessness). The
    framework reviews now underway are rewriting the lessons in
    real time.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The frontier.</strong> Central bank digital currencies (CBDCs) would
    give the public direct central-bank money — a run-risk question, a
    privacy question and a monetary-policy question at once. Climate
    mandates are pulling banks into credit allocation (contested
    territory for institutions built on market neutrality). And the
    independence consensus itself is under political pressure in
    several countries — the 1970s lesson is one generation old, and
    institutional memory decays.
  </p>
